From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000022 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Port Bot <port@local>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 15:00:00 +0200
Subject: 0022: enforce the console's origin on websocket upgrades

Three gaps let a page on another origin open websockets through bridge
with the user's session cookie (sent for same-site origins, e.g. another
app under the same parent domain, despite SameSite=Strict):

- csrfverifier.verifyCSRF returned early for GET before its websocket
  source-origin check, so that check never ran (handshakes are always
  GET). /api/graphql's upgrader accepts every origin, so GraphQL
  websockets had no origin check at all. Websocket upgrades on
  authenticated routes are now checked against --base-address before the
  safe-method shortcut.

- pkg/proxy dialed the backend before running CheckOrigin, so a refused
  upgrade still reached the backend, which acts on it right away (the
  apiserver starts a pods/exec session once dialed), despite 0014's k8s
  proxy origin check. The origin is now checked before dialing.

- Plugin proxies (--plugin-proxy) never set Config.Origin, and their
  HeaderBlacklist strips Origin before CheckOrigin read it, so setting it
  would have refused everything. The Origin is now read before the
  blacklist is applied, and plugin proxies get the same --base-address
  derived origin as the k8s proxy, which also covers authorize:false
  routes that skip the auth middleware.
